ext{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-6\\int {e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-6J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{6x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6{e}^{6x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{6x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(6{e}^{6x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+6\\int {e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+6I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I.$ We remember to add a constant of integration $C$ at the end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-6J \\\\ I & ={e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-6(-{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+6I) \\\\ I & ={e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+6{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-36I \\\\ 37I & ={e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+6{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{6x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{37}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535059 true] [17592186328919 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328920 13194139535059 true] [17592186328919 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328921 13194139535059 true] [17592186328919 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328922 13194139535059 true] [17592186328919 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328923 13194139535059 true] [17592186328919 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328924 13194139535059 true] [17592186328919 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535059